# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug/
target/

# Cargo.lock is tracked because this is an executable
# More information here https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/guide/cargo-toml-vs-cargo-lock.html

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb
./.idea/*
target_ide/
**/node_modules/
# Runtime state, not fixtures.
#
# `BackendType::Filesystem("filesystem")` in the test suite writes account
# records, transfers and the virtual FS here. Eleven of those files were
# committed -- six .hca account records at ~49KB each, which is serialized
# post-quantum key material for throwaway test accounts.
#
# Two costs, both paid repeatedly. Every `cargo nextest run` rewrote them, so
# the tree was dirty after any test run and a `git add -A` would sweep them into
# an unrelated commit. And each run started from whatever state the last one
# left, which is the opposite of what a fixture is for.
#
# Nothing reads these paths: the suite passes with the directory deleted
# outright (277/277). They were never fixtures, only residue.
citadel-internal-service/filesystem/

# The WASM source stamp that sync-wasm-clients.sh drops into every destination
# it copies to.
#
# Only ONE copy is authoritative: the parent repo's
# `citadel-workspace-client-ts/pkg/.wasm-source-tree`, which is tracked and is
# the one `check-wasm-matches-its-source.mjs` reads. This copy is written and
# never read. Tracking it would be a second source for the same fact, free to
# disagree with the first; leaving it untracked makes every sync leave a dirty
# tree, which is how residue ends up in an unrelated `git add -A`.
typescript-client/.wasm-source-tree
